1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,560
Right now, around eighty six
billion nerve cells

2
00:00:03,570 --> 00:00:06,250
in your brain are firing tiny
electrical signals,

3
00:00:06,650 --> 00:00:08,430
thousands of times a second.

4
00:00:09,167 --> 00:00:11,917
Every thought, every memory,
every movement you make,

5
00:00:11,927 --> 00:00:13,607
is written in those signals.

6
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:17,220
And it does all of this on about
twenty watts of power,

7
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:18,880
less than a dim light bulb.

8
00:00:19,667 --> 00:00:22,727
For most of history, that code
was completely private.

9
00:00:23,207 --> 00:00:26,477
Now, scientists are building
machines that can read it,

10
00:00:26,487 --> 00:00:27,807
and turn it into action.

11
00:00:28,750 --> 00:00:31,770
People who cannot move or speak
are typing,

12
00:00:32,210 --> 00:00:34,350
talking and moving robotic arms,

13
00:00:35,050 --> 00:00:35,890
just by thinking.

14
00:00:36,490 --> 00:00:39,250
This is the story of the machine
that can read your brain.

15
00:00:40,983 --> 00:00:44,393
In seventeen ninety one, the
Italian scientist

16
00:00:44,403 --> 00:00:47,713
Luigi Galvani made a dead frog's
leg kick,

17
00:00:47,723 --> 00:00:49,063
using electricity.

18
00:00:49,843 --> 00:00:53,343
Nerves and muscles, it turned
out, run on electrical signals.

19
00:00:54,167 --> 00:00:57,557
In nineteen twenty four, a
German psychiatrist named

20
00:00:57,567 --> 00:01:00,537
Hans Berger stuck electrodes on
his patients' heads,

21
00:01:00,547 --> 00:01:03,987
and recorded faint waves of
electricity from the brain
itself.

22
00:01:04,917 --> 00:01:08,367
He was so unsure of his strange
results that he kept them

23
00:01:08,377 --> 00:01:11,827
to himself for five years,
before finally publishing

24
00:01:11,837 --> 00:01:13,987
in nineteen twenty nine.

25
00:01:13,997 --> 00:01:15,917
Many scientists didn't believe
him.

26
00:01:16,583 --> 00:01:19,733
He called it the
electroencephalogram, or

27
00:01:19,743 --> 00:01:20,413
EEG.

28
00:01:20,423 --> 00:01:23,423
For the first time, we could
watch a living brain at work.

29
00:01:24,167 --> 00:01:27,397
But EEG is like listening to a
stadium crowd from

30
00:01:27,407 --> 00:01:28,207
outside:

31
00:01:28,727 --> 00:01:31,327
you can hear the roar, but not
individual conversations.

32
00:01:32,583 --> 00:01:34,283
To read the brain in detail,

33
00:01:34,903 --> 00:01:37,353
scientists needed to listen to
single neurons,

34
00:01:37,363 --> 00:01:38,203
up close.

35
00:01:39,083 --> 00:01:42,353
In nineteen seventy three, a
computer scientist at

36
00:01:42,363 --> 00:01:43,163
UCLA,

37
00:01:43,483 --> 00:01:46,793
Jacques Vidal, coined the phrase
brain-computer interface,

38
00:01:46,803 --> 00:01:50,083
and asked whether brain signals
could control machines.

39
00:01:51,167 --> 00:01:54,597
The first brain-computer
interfaces most people have
heard of

40
00:01:54,607 --> 00:01:55,827
don't read thoughts at all.

41
00:01:56,107 --> 00:01:59,497
Cochlear implants turn sound
into signals for the hearing

42
00:01:59,507 --> 00:02:00,307
nerve,

43
00:02:00,367 --> 00:02:02,467
and over a million people now
use them.

44
00:02:03,983 --> 00:02:07,393
A strip of brain called the
motor cortex controls

45
00:02:07,403 --> 00:02:08,203
movement.

46
00:02:08,563 --> 00:02:11,123
Each part of it handles a
different part of the body:

47
00:02:11,583 --> 00:02:12,883
hands, lips, legs.

48
00:02:13,833 --> 00:02:17,233
In the nineteen eighties, the
neuroscientist Apostolos

49
00:02:17,243 --> 00:02:20,303
Georgopoulos showed that when a
monkey moves its

50
00:02:20,313 --> 00:02:21,113
arm,

51
00:02:21,553 --> 00:02:23,533
groups of neurons vote on the
direction.

52
00:02:24,113 --> 00:02:26,553
Average their votes, and you can
predict the movement.

53
00:02:27,417 --> 00:02:30,727
The key point: those signals
keep firing even in

54
00:02:30,737 --> 00:02:31,957
people who are paralysed.

55
00:02:32,697 --> 00:02:34,377
The brain still sends the
command.

56
00:02:35,037 --> 00:02:36,397
It just never reaches the
muscles.

57
00:02:37,417 --> 00:02:39,747
So what if you could intercept
the command,

58
00:02:39,757 --> 00:02:41,677
and send it somewhere else?

59
00:02:42,583 --> 00:02:45,853
In nineteen ninety eight, the
neurologist Phil Kennedy

60
00:02:45,863 --> 00:02:49,113
implanted an electrode in a
paralysed man named

61
00:02:49,123 --> 00:02:52,563
Johnny Ray, who learned to move
a cursor with his brain signals.

62
00:02:53,333 --> 00:02:56,523
In two thousand and eight,
monkeys at the University

63
00:02:56,533 --> 00:02:59,923
of Pittsburgh fed themselves
marshmallows using

64
00:02:59,933 --> 00:03:03,273
a robotic arm, controlled
directly by their brains.

65
00:03:05,150 --> 00:03:08,440
The tool for that job is a tiny
chip called the Utah

66
00:03:08,450 --> 00:03:09,250
array:

67
00:03:09,490 --> 00:03:12,700
four millimetres across, with a
hundred hair-thin needles

68
00:03:12,710 --> 00:03:14,810
that sit in the brain's surface.

69
00:03:15,750 --> 00:03:18,820
It was invented by the
bioengineer Richard Normann at
the

70
00:03:18,830 --> 00:03:22,320
University of Utah, and versions
of it are still used

71
00:03:22,330 --> 00:03:24,510
in most of the patients you're
about to meet.

72
00:03:25,250 --> 00:03:28,110
In two thousand and four,
Matthew Nagle,

73
00:03:28,550 --> 00:03:31,840
a young man paralysed from the
neck down after a knife

74
00:03:31,850 --> 00:03:32,650
attack,

75
00:03:32,710 --> 00:03:35,360
had one implanted, in a research
trial called

76
00:03:35,370 --> 00:03:36,245
BrainGate.

77
00:03:36,750 --> 00:03:40,120
Just by imagining moving his
hand, he could move a cursor

78
00:03:40,130 --> 00:03:43,300
on a screen, open emails, and
play a simple

79
00:03:43,310 --> 00:03:44,110
video game.

80
00:03:44,917 --> 00:03:48,077
In twenty twelve, a woman named
Cathy Hutchinson,

81
00:03:48,517 --> 00:03:51,237
paralysed by a stroke fifteen
years earlier,

82
00:03:51,717 --> 00:03:55,027
used a BrainGate implant to
control a robotic arm,

83
00:03:55,037 --> 00:03:57,837
and lift a bottle of coffee to
her lips.

84
00:03:58,750 --> 00:04:01,860
It was the first time in fifteen
years she had served

85
00:04:01,870 --> 00:04:02,870
herself a drink.

86
00:04:03,583 --> 00:04:05,643
Signals can flow the other way
too.

87
00:04:06,263 --> 00:04:09,513
In twenty sixteen, Nathan
Copeland, paralysed

88
00:04:09,523 --> 00:04:12,893
in a car crash, felt pressure in
his fingers

89
00:04:12,903 --> 00:04:16,233
when a robotic hand was touched,
thanks to tiny

90
00:04:16,243 --> 00:04:18,123
pulses sent into his brain.

91
00:04:18,703 --> 00:04:21,163
He later fist-bumped President
Obama with it.

92
00:04:22,567 --> 00:04:24,267
Then the machines learned to
write.

93
00:04:24,907 --> 00:04:27,937
In twenty twenty one, a Stanford
team asked a

94
00:04:27,947 --> 00:04:31,287
paralysed man to imagine writing
letters by hand.

95
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,190
An AI decoder turned his
imagined pen strokes into

96
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:38,660
text on a screen, at around
ninety characters a minute,

97
00:04:39,020 --> 00:04:41,300
close to the speed of texting on
a phone.

98
00:04:42,167 --> 00:04:43,207
Speech came next.

99
00:04:43,927 --> 00:04:46,777
In twenty twenty three, two
teams in California

100
00:04:46,787 --> 00:04:50,177
decoded attempted speech,
directly from the brains

101
00:04:50,187 --> 00:04:53,287
of women who had lost the
ability to speak.

102
00:04:54,083 --> 00:04:54,883
Pat Bennett,

103
00:04:55,163 --> 00:04:55,963
who has ALS,

104
00:04:56,503 --> 00:04:59,223
a disease that slowly paralyses
the body,

105
00:04:59,563 --> 00:05:02,323
could communicate at about sixty
words a minute.

106
00:05:02,783 --> 00:05:06,173
A second woman, Ann Johnson,
paralysed by

107
00:05:06,183 --> 00:05:09,373
a stroke, spoke through a
digital avatar on

108
00:05:09,383 --> 00:05:10,183
screen.

109
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:13,910
In twenty twenty four, a team at
UC Davis gave

110
00:05:13,920 --> 00:05:17,140
a man with ALS a voice that
sounded like his own,

111
00:05:17,740 --> 00:05:19,280
recreated from old recordings.

112
00:05:20,060 --> 00:05:22,700
When he first heard it, he and
his family wept.

113
00:05:24,233 --> 00:05:26,073
Private companies have joined
the race.

114
00:05:26,833 --> 00:05:27,633
One of them,

115
00:05:27,993 --> 00:05:31,203
Neuralink, founded by Elon Musk,
uses a robot

116
00:05:31,213 --> 00:05:33,733
to sew ultra-thin threads into
the brain.

117
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:37,910
Its implant, about the size of a
coin, sits in a

118
00:05:37,920 --> 00:05:40,970
hole in the skull and records
from more than a thousand

119
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:41,550
electrodes,

120
00:05:41,560 --> 00:05:43,920
spread across sixty four
threads.

121
00:05:44,833 --> 00:05:47,233
In twenty twenty four, its first
patient,

122
00:05:47,653 --> 00:05:50,903
Noland Arbaugh, paralysed in a
diving accident,

123
00:05:50,913 --> 00:05:54,163
used his implant to play online
chess and

124
00:05:54,173 --> 00:05:57,153
video games, controlling the
cursor with his thoughts.

125
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,490
Another company, Synchron,
avoids open brain

126
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:02,300
surgery.

127
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:06,130
Its device is a tiny mesh tube,
threaded up through a blood

128
00:06:06,140 --> 00:06:09,580
vessel from the neck, to sit
right next to the motor cortex.

129
00:06:10,667 --> 00:06:13,387
Most implants still need a cable
through the skull,

130
00:06:13,807 --> 00:06:15,087
connected to a computer.

131
00:06:15,607 --> 00:06:17,047
Newer devices are wireless,

132
00:06:17,987 --> 00:06:19,427
charging through the skin like a
phone.

133
00:06:20,250 --> 00:06:23,740
Patients with Synchron implants
have sent texts and

134
00:06:23,750 --> 00:06:24,000
emails,

135
00:06:24,010 --> 00:06:26,730
and shopped online, by thought
alone.

136
00:06:27,583 --> 00:06:30,813
In twenty twenty three, a Dutch
man named Gert-Jan

137
00:06:30,823 --> 00:06:31,623
Oskam,

138
00:06:32,043 --> 00:06:34,903
paralysed in a cycling accident,
walked again,

139
00:06:35,343 --> 00:06:38,653
using implants that read his
brain's intentions and

140
00:06:38,663 --> 00:06:41,933
passed them wirelessly to a
stimulator on his spinal

141
00:06:41,943 --> 00:06:42,743
cord.

142
00:06:42,923 --> 00:06:44,883
The team called it a digital
bridge.

143
00:06:45,833 --> 00:06:46,733
Similar implants,

144
00:06:47,353 --> 00:06:50,743
called deep brain stimulators,
are already used by

145
00:06:50,753 --> 00:06:53,213
more than a hundred and sixty
thousand people,

146
00:06:53,553 --> 00:06:56,593
mostly to calm the tremors of
Parkinson's disease.

147
00:06:58,483 --> 00:07:00,463
What about reading thoughts
without surgery?

148
00:07:01,023 --> 00:07:04,393
Brain scanners called fMRI can
see which

149
00:07:04,403 --> 00:07:07,803
areas of the brain are working,
by tracking blood flow.

150
00:07:08,583 --> 00:07:11,973
As early as twenty eleven, a
Berkeley team used

151
00:07:11,983 --> 00:07:15,373
fMRI to make blurry
reconstructions of

152
00:07:15,383 --> 00:07:17,683
movie clips that volunteers were
watching.

153
00:07:18,500 --> 00:07:21,690
In twenty twenty three, a team
at the University

154
00:07:21,700 --> 00:07:24,790
of Texas trained an AI on many

155
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:27,970
hours of a volunteer's brain
scans as they

156
00:07:27,980 --> 00:07:29,220
listened to podcasts.

157
00:07:30,020 --> 00:07:33,280
It could then produce the rough
gist of new stories they heard,

158
00:07:33,780 --> 00:07:34,580
or even imagined.

159
00:07:35,333 --> 00:07:38,543
It only worked on people who had
spent hours training

160
00:07:38,553 --> 00:07:38,943
it,

161
00:07:38,953 --> 00:07:40,333
and who cooperated.

162
00:07:40,833 --> 00:07:43,453
When volunteers thought about
something else on purpose,

163
00:07:44,053 --> 00:07:44,853
it failed.

164
00:07:45,053 --> 00:07:47,313
True mind reading is still
science fiction.

165
00:07:49,067 --> 00:07:50,367
But it raises new questions.

166
00:07:51,147 --> 00:07:52,547
Who owns the data from your
brain?

167
00:07:53,107 --> 00:07:56,107
Could it be sold, hacked, or
used against you?

168
00:07:56,750 --> 00:08:00,140
In twenty twenty one, Chile
became the first country

169
00:08:00,150 --> 00:08:03,190
to protect brain activity in its
constitution.

170
00:08:04,070 --> 00:08:07,000
In twenty twenty four, the US
state of Colorado

171
00:08:07,010 --> 00:08:09,450
passed a law protecting neural
data.

172
00:08:10,167 --> 00:08:13,107
Implants also carry the risks of
brain surgery,

173
00:08:13,727 --> 00:08:15,947
and some early devices have had
problems.

174
00:08:16,667 --> 00:08:19,877
In Neuralink's first patient,
many of the threads pulled

175
00:08:19,887 --> 00:08:23,297
back out of the brain within
weeks, although the team
recovered

176
00:08:23,307 --> 00:08:25,387
much of the performance with
software.

177
00:08:27,233 --> 00:08:30,583
A century ago, Hans Berger could
barely detect a

178
00:08:30,593 --> 00:08:31,953
whisper of brain electricity.

179
00:08:33,083 --> 00:08:36,353
Today, machines can turn the
signals in a paralysed

180
00:08:36,363 --> 00:08:39,753
person's brain into words,
movement and a

181
00:08:39,763 --> 00:08:40,563
voice.

182
00:08:40,833 --> 00:08:43,173
The machine that can read your
brain isn't coming.

183
00:08:43,533 --> 00:08:46,563
For a small number of people,
it's already here,

184
00:08:46,573 --> 00:08:48,553
and it's giving them back their
lives.
